# A HOLOGRAPHIC QUANTUM ERROR-CORRECTING CODE, the discrete realization of the AdS/CFT correspondence, modeled and proved
# CORRECT by the kernel. In a holographic code (the HaPPY pentagon code of Pastawski, Yoshida, Harlow, and Preskill) a
# few BULK logical degrees of freedom are encoded into many BOUNDARY physical qubits by a hyperbolic tiling of perfect
# tensors. The defining miracle is SUBREGION DUALITY: the bulk information is RECONSTRUCTIBLE from any sufficiently large
# region of the boundary, and equivalently the ERASURE of a small boundary region leaves the bulk untouched and
# recoverable from the complement. This is the discrete face of the Ryu-Takayanagi entanglement wedge: a boundary region
# reconstructs exactly the bulk that lies in its wedge, and a boundary region holds enough of the bulk to correct the
# loss of any region smaller than itself.
#
# We model the provable core on a boundary of THREE physical bits carrying ONE bulk logical bit, the same redundancy as
# the three-bit repetition code, but read through the holographic lens. The bulk bit is encoded by spreading it across
# the boundary; a MAJORITY vote over the boundary reconstructs it; ERASING any single boundary position (replacing it
# with a default) still leaves a majority of the original, so the bulk survives; and any boundary REGION that holds a
# majority of the physical bits reconstructs the bulk on its own, which is the entanglement-wedge threshold. The kernel
# proves, by exhaustive induction over the bulk bit and over each erasure position and each majority region, that the
# bulk reconstructs after no loss, after ANY single erasure, and from EVERY majority region. The radial bulk-boundary
# wedge of `code/space/holography.tree` supplies the geometry; this file supplies the code living on that boundary.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# A bit, off or on. A bulk LOGICAL bit and each BOUNDARY PHYSICAL bit are both of this type.
form bit
  case off
  case on

# Equality of two bits, yes exactly when they match. Used by majority reconstruction to compare boundary positions.
task same
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case off
      fork case, read b
        case off
          send back
            make yes
        case on
          send back
            make no
    case on
      fork case, read b
        case off
          send back
            make no
        case on
          send back
            make yes

# THE BOUNDARY: three physical bits, the conformal-field-theory degrees of freedom into which the single bulk bit is
# holographically spread. Each position is one boundary site of the discrete hyperbolic boundary.
form boundary
  case sites
    link one, like bit
    link two, like bit
    link three, like bit

# ENCODE the bulk logical bit into the boundary, spreading it across all three sites: off goes to off-off-off, on goes to
# on-on-on. This is the discrete bulk-to-boundary map, the isometry that pushes the bulk degree of freedom out to the
# boundary so that it is redundantly recorded everywhere.
task encode
  take b, like bit
  like boundary
  send back
    make sites
      bind one
        read b
      bind two
        read b
      bind three
        read b

# DECODE the boundary back to the bulk bit by MAJORITY vote, the value held by at least two of the three sites. If the
# first two agree that is the bulk value; otherwise the third breaks the tie. This is the global boundary-to-bulk
# reconstruction map, reading the bulk out of the full boundary.
task decode
  take w, like boundary
  like bit
  fork case, read w
    case sites
      link one
      link two
      link three
      fork case, read one
        case off
          fork case, read two
            case off
              send back
                make off
            case on
              send back
                read three
        case on
          fork case, read two
            case off
              send back
                read three
            case on
              send back
                make on

# A POSITION on the boundary, naming which physical site an erasure removes or which the entanglement wedge of a region
# omits. The three sites of the boundary triple.
form position
  case at-one
  case at-two
  case at-three

# TEMPLATE: one bound site in the rebuilt boundary. `hold` passes the site straight through (it survives the erasure);
# `gone` resets it to the default off (it was lost to the environment). One pair of templates removes the repeated
# `read` and `make off` lines from the three erasure branches below.
tree hold
  take name

  hook fuse
    read {name}

tree gone
  take name

  hook fuse
    make off

# ERASE one boundary site at a position, the holographic erasure channel: a physical qubit is lost to the environment and
# replaced by the default value off (the maximally-mixed erasure flag, modeled here as a fixed reset). The other two sites
# survive untouched, so a majority of the original boundary remains and the bulk is unharmed. Each branch rebuilds the
# boundary with one site `gone` and the other two `hold`.
task erase-at
  take w, like boundary
  take p, like position
  like boundary
  fork case, read w
    case sites
      link one
      link two
      link three
      fork case, read p
        case at-one
          send back
            make sites
              bind one
                fuse gone, one
              bind two
                fuse hold, two
              bind three
                fuse hold, three
        case at-two
          send back
            make sites
              bind one
                fuse hold, one
              bind two
                fuse gone, two
              bind three
                fuse hold, three
        case at-three
          send back
            make sites
              bind one
                fuse hold, one
              bind two
                fuse hold, two
              bind three
                fuse gone, three

# A boundary REGION, the part of the boundary an observer holds, named by which sites it CONTAINS. A region with at least
# two of the three sites is a MAJORITY region whose entanglement wedge reaches the bulk bit. We enumerate the three
# two-site majority regions (each omits exactly one site) and the full boundary.
form region
  case keep-one-two
  case keep-two-three
  case keep-one-three
  case keep-all

# RECONSTRUCT the bulk bit FROM A REGION, the heart of subregion duality. A majority region holds at least two sites that
# AGREE (since the original boundary was uniform, the two surviving sites still carry the bulk value), so the region reads
# the bulk out from the two sites it keeps. For each two-site region the two kept sites determine the bulk value, and the
# full boundary falls back to the ordinary majority decode. The omitted site is the part of the boundary outside the
# wedge, never consulted.
task reconstruct-from-region
  take w, like boundary
  take g, like region
  like bit
  fork case, read w
    case sites
      link one
      link two
      link three
      fork case, read g
        case keep-one-two
          fork case, read one
            case off
              send back
                make off
            case on
              send back
                read two
        case keep-two-three
          fork case, read two
            case off
              send back
                make off
            case on
              send back
                read three
        case keep-one-three
          fork case, read one
            case off
              send back
                make off
            case on
              send back
                read three
        case keep-all
          send back
            call decode
              read w

# THE BULK RECONSTRUCTS FROM THE BOUNDARY: decode (encode b) = b, for both bulk values. Spreading the bulk bit out to the
# boundary and reading it back by majority returns the original. This is the bulk-to-boundary isometry composed with the
# global reconstruction map, the identity on the bulk. Proved by induction over the bulk bit.
rule bulk-reconstructs-from-the-boundary
  seat b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call decode
        call encode
          read b
      read b
  fold b

# SINGLE ERASURE IS CORRECTABLE AT POSITION ONE: decode (erase-at (encode b) at-one) = b, for both bulk values. Losing the
# first boundary site to the environment leaves the other two carrying the bulk value, a majority of two, so the bulk is
# recovered untouched. This is the holographic erasure-correction property at the first site.
rule single-erasure-correctable-at-one
  seat b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call decode
        call erase-at
          call encode
            read b
          make at-one
      read b
  fold b

# SINGLE ERASURE IS CORRECTABLE AT POSITION TWO.
rule single-erasure-correctable-at-two
  seat b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call decode
        call erase-at
          call encode
            read b
          make at-two
      read b
  fold b

# SINGLE ERASURE IS CORRECTABLE AT POSITION THREE. With the two above, ANY single boundary erasure is corrected: the loss
# of one physical qubit anywhere on the boundary leaves the bulk recoverable from the complement, the defining property of
# a holographic code. The complementary region, a majority of two, contains the bulk in its entanglement wedge.
rule single-erasure-correctable-at-three
  seat b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call decode
        call erase-at
          call encode
            read b
          make at-three
      read b
  fold b

# RECONSTRUCTION FROM A MAJORITY REGION: reconstruct-from-region (encode b) g = b, for every bulk value and every majority
# region g. Any boundary region holding at least two sites reconstructs the bulk on its own, never consulting the omitted
# site. This is subregion duality: the bulk bit lies in the entanglement wedge of every majority region, so each such
# region recovers it independently. Proved by induction over the bulk bit and over the four regions.
rule reconstruction-from-a-majority-region
  seat b, like bit
  seat g, like region
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reconstruct-from-region
        call encode
          read b
        read g
      read b
  fold b, g

# THE WEDGE OF A REGION AGREES WITH GLOBAL DECODE: reconstructing the bulk from the full boundary region equals the global
# majority decode, reconstruct-from-region w keep-all = decode w. The full boundary is its own entanglement wedge, so its
# subregion reconstruction is the global one. Tying the region-restricted map to `decode` mirrors the wedge of the center
# in `code/space/holography.tree`, where the central bulk point governs the whole boundary. Proved over every boundary.
rule full-region-reconstruction-is-global-decode
  seat x, like bit
  seat y, like bit
  seat z, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reconstruct-from-region
        make sites
          bind one
            read x
          bind two
            read y
          bind three
            read z
        make keep-all
      call decode
        make sites
          bind one
            read x
          bind two
            read y
          bind three
            read z
  fold x, y, z

# Read the first boundary site, used to witness the erasure reset.
task site-one-of
  take w, like boundary
  like bit
  fork case, read w
    case sites
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        read one

# AN ERASED SITE READS AS THE DEFAULT: erasing position one then reading the first site gives off, the reset value, for
# every boundary. This confirms the erasure channel really removes the site (replaces it with the default off) rather than
# leaving it in place, so the recovery proofs above are correcting a genuine loss. Proved over every boundary.
rule erasure-resets-the-lost-site
  seat x, like bit
  seat y, like bit
  seat z, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call site-one-of
        call erase-at
          make sites
            bind one
              read x
            bind two
              read y
            bind three
              read z
          make at-one
      make off
  fold x, y, z
